<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Ilyasid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">932 CE – 968 CE · capital Bardasir</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived dynasty founded by a Samanid general that ruled the province of Kerman in south-eastern Iran for nearly forty years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-ilyas">Abu Ali Muhammad ibn Ilyas</a>, a commander of Sogdian origin in the service of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/samanid-empire">Samanid Empire</a>, made himself master of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> around 932. He ruled from Bardasir, the forerunner of the modern city of Kerman, nominally recognising the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-ilyas">Muhammad ibn Ilyas</a> held the province against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty</a> and local tribes, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baloch">Baluch</a> and Qufs of the mountains. His rule was largely military, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman's</a> location between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khurasan</a> made it valuable for trade and tribute. Disputes between his sons, Alyasa and Ilyas, weakened the family after he retired.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 968 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a>, ending the dynasty. Alyasa fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanid</a> territory.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ilyasid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Ilyasid dynasty" width="320" height="221"><figcaption>Image: Arab_Hafez, edited by HistoryofIran (me). · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BanuIlyasMapHistoryofIran.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>932 CE – 968 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bardasir</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Muhammad ibn Ilyas, Alyasa ibn Muhammad</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ilyasids, Banu Ilyas</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=950&amp;focus=ilyasid-dynasty" aria-label="Ilyasid dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6150 1085 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ilyasid dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6470 1341L6588 1010M6470 1341L6367 1352"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6588 1010h0M6367 1352h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6470 1341h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6470 1341h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bardasir. <a href="/atlas?year=950&amp;focus=ilyasid-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

